// Chapter: Graphics in ICT | Difficulty: hard
::Crop to Content::
In image manipulation software like GIMP, how does "Crop to Content" differ from "Crop to Selection"?
[difficulty:hard]
[chapter:Graphics in ICT]
[tags:gimp,cropping]
{
    =Crop to Content automatically detects the main subject and removes empty background areas without manual selection, whereas Crop to Selection crops strictly to user-defined boundaries
    ~Crop to Content requires a manual selection tool, whereas Crop to Selection is fully automated by GIMP
    ~Crop to Content expands the boundaries of the canvas, whereas Crop to Selection reduces them
    ~Crop to Content changes the file format of the image, whereas Crop to Selection changes the resolution
}

// Chapter: Graphics in ICT | Difficulty: hard
::Shear Tool vs Skew::
Which image transformation tool slants/tilts an image horizontally or vertically while strictly maintaining its original dimensions, parallel lines, and fixed boundaries without expanding the canvas?
[difficulty:hard]
[chapter:Graphics in ICT]
[tags:gimp,transformations]
{
    =Shear
    ~Skew
    ~Perspective
    ~Rotate
}

// Chapter: Digital Documentation | Difficulty: hard
::Spreadsheet Sorting::
In spreadsheets, how are text entries starting with special characters or numbers handled during a standard ascending sort?
[difficulty:hard]
[chapter:Digital Documentation]
[tags:spreadsheet,sorting]
{
    =They are sorted and displayed before entries starting with alphabetical letters
    ~They are completely ignored and hidden from the table view
    ~They are placed at the very end of the sorted list, after alphabetical letters
    ~They cause an error flag because they do not contain text letters
}

// Chapter: Programming | Difficulty: hard
::Scratch Stage Boundaries::
What are the total width, height, and coordinate ranges of the standard Scratch stage?
[difficulty:hard]
[chapter:Programming]
[tags:scratch,coordinates]
{
    =The stage size is \(480 \times 360\) pixels, where \(x\) ranges from \(-240\) to \(240\), and \(y\) ranges from \(-180\) to \(180\)
    ~The stage size is \(1000 \times 1000\) pixels, where \(x\) ranges from \(0\) to \(1000\), and \(y\) ranges from \(0\) to \(1000\)
    ~The stage size is \(360 \times 240\) pixels, where \(x\) ranges from \(-180\) to \(180\), and \(y\) ranges from \(-120\) to \(120\)
    ~The stage size is \(640 \times 480\) pixels, where \(x\) ranges from \(-320\) to \(320\), and \(y\) ranges from \(-240\) to \(240\)
}

